== verifier note on the P4 "DISAGREES" line ==
The emulation in P4 was run on argv[0] alone (65 61 5f 80), where the trailing 0x80 has no following byte and the
emulation emits U+FFFD and stops. The real transcoder does not see argv[0] alone: recreateCommandLine() joins argv with
single spaces first (utils.h:1293-1302), so the byte after 0x80 is the join space 0x20. On the joined string the same
algorithm gives ((0x80&0x1F)<<6)|(0x20&0x3F) = 0x20 < 0x80 -> U+FFFD and consumes the space, which is exactly what was
observed: token[0] = "ea_�-q", token count 5. So P4 AGREES with the mechanism; the DISAGREES label is an artefact
of what the emulation was fed, not of the tool. (Left in place rather than edited: this log is append-only.)

== verifier reading of the objections (REFUTE lens) ==
stale binary?            NO  - sha256 1ad1568c... matches the README and the production binary at run time.
shell/quoting artefact?  NO  - P1-P6 were launched from python with bytes argv and no shell; same result as the bash exec -a run.
escape-only behaviour?   NO  - P2 wrote an errorLevel=-1 report with NO EA_ALLOW_SHORT_DATASET and before any sample was read;
                               the commandline token is mangled identically. The field is captured at non_iid_main.cpp:113 and
                               serialised by the same Json::StyledWriter in every report (non_iid_test_run.h:26).
parsing mistake?         NO  - the raw bytes of the "commandline" line are logged: 65 61 5f 5c 75 66 66 66 64 71 = ea_�q; no 5a.
direction holds?         YES - neutral: sha256 field equals the fixture's sha256 in every data run; no estimator value is touched.
production reachability: needs a byte >= 0x80 that is not valid UTF-8 somewhere in argv. On this host APFS refuses such a
                         name for the input (P7: EILSEQ) and would refuse it for -o too, so only argv[0] (exec -a) reaches it
                         here, which no production invocation does. On ext4 (CI x86-64) the input filename can carry it; not measured.

== mechanism confirmed from jsoncpp 1.9.8 SOURCE (fetched read-only: raw.githubusercontent.com/open-source-parsers/jsoncpp/1.9.8/src/lib_json/json_writer.cpp) ==
StyledWriter::writeValue, case stringValue:  pushValue(valueToQuotedStringN(str, static_cast<size_t>(end - str)));   // emitUTF8 defaults to false
valueToQuotedStringN(..., bool emitUTF8 = false) default branch:  unsigned codepoint = utf8ToCodepoint(c, end); ... else if (codepoint < 0x10000) appendHex(result, codepoint);
utf8ToCodepoint:  if (firstByte < 0x80) return firstByte;
                  if (firstByte < 0xE0) { if (e - s < 2) return REPLACEMENT_CHARACTER;
                        unsigned int calculated = ((firstByte & 0x1F) << 6) | (static_cast<unsigned int>(s[1]) & 0x3F);
                        s += 1;   return calculated < 0x80 ? REPLACEMENT_CHARACTER : calculated; }
                  if (firstByte < 0xF0) { ... s[1], s[2] ... s += 2; ... return calculated < 0x800 ? REPLACEMENT_CHARACTER : calculated; }
                  ...
The `firstByte < 0xE0` branch admits 0x80..0xBF (continuation bytes, which can never lead a sequence) and 0xC0/0xC1 (overlong leads)
as 2-byte leads and never checks that s[1] is a continuation byte (0x80..0xBF). Arithmetic for the measured cases:
  80 5A      -> ((0x80&0x1F)<<6)|(0x5A&0x3F) = 0x1A < 0x80 -> U+FFFD, s advanced past 'Z'            (P1, P2: observed �, 'Z' gone)
  80 20      -> 0x20 < 0x80 -> U+FFFD, s advanced past the join space                                   (P4: observed "ea_�-q", 5 tokens)
  E9 65 73   -> ((0xE9&0x0F)<<12)|((0x65&0x3F)<<6)|(0x73&0x3F) = 0x9000|0x940|0x33 = 0x9973 -> 饳 (P3: observed 饳, NO U+FFFD, 'e' and 's' gone)
  C3 A9      -> ((0xC3&0x1F)<<6)|(0xA9&0x3F) = 0xC0|0x29 = 0xE9 -> é                               (P5: lossless, as the reproducer says valid UTF-8 is)
  FF / FE    -> firstByte >= 0xF8 -> REPLACEMENT_CHARACTER, s not advanced -> two �                 (README u2 case)

